    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d78-a1">

                <pb facs="0319" n="284" xml:id="pg284"/>


                <head><hi rendition="#bold">Draft Letter From President Carter to Egyptian President
                        Sadat</hi><anchor n="7" xml:id="d78-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1978-10-02T23:59:59-04:00"
                            notBefore="1978-09-30T13:00:00-04:00">undated</date>
                    </dateline>
                    <salute>Dear Mr. President:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>Now that the Israeli Knesset has approved the “Framework for the Conclusion of a
                    Peace Treaty Between Egypt and Israel,” as well as the withdrawal of all Israeli
                    settlers from Sinai according to a timetable within the period specified for the
                    implementation of the peace treaty, I believe we should proceed to begin the
                    talks to conclude peace between Egypt and Israel.</p>
                <p>I would like therefore to<anchor n="8" xml:id="d78-f2"/> extend to the Government
                    of Egypt an invitation to send its Foreign and Defense Ministers<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d78-f3"/> to Washington for talks to begin on October 12 with their
                    Israeli counterparts. I am today sending this same invitation to Prime Minister
                    Begin.</p>
                <p>I have asked Secretary Vance to represent the United States at these talks, which
                    we are prepared to arrange to be held at an appropriate site in Washington.</p>
                <p>With all best wishes,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Jimmy Carter</hi><anchor n="10" xml:id="d78-f4"
                        /></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d78-f1" n="7" type="source" xml:id="d78-f1-content">
                <p>No classification marking. The letter as amended by Carter was sent to Cairo on
                    October 2. (Telegram 249940 to Cairo, October 2; National Archives, RG 59,
                    Central Foreign Policy File, D780402–0561) Eilts reported in telegram 22019 from
                    Cairo, October 2, that the letter was delivered to the office of Boutros-Ghali
                    for couriering to Sadat at The Barrages. (National Archives, RG 59, Central
                    Foreign Policy File, D780402–0902) Boutros Ghali informed Eilts on October 3
                    that Sadat had instructed him to accept formally the invitation. The Egyptian
                    foreign ministry previously informed the Egyptian media of Sadat’s acceptance
                    the night of October 2. (Telegram 22050 from Cairo, October 3, National
                    Archives, RG 59, Central Foreign Policy File, D780403–1090)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d78-f2" n="8" xml:id="d78-f2-content">
                <p>Carter struck through “would like” and “to” in this phrase.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d78-f3" n="9" xml:id="d78-f3-content">
                <p>Carter crossed out the phrase “its Foreign and Defense Ministers” and replaced it
                    with “your negotiating team.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d78-f4" n="10" xml:id="d78-f4-content">
                <p>Carter wrote: “OK. J” next to this typewritten signature.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
